## INTRODUCTION
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with amidic linkers have been shown to exhibit rich redox chemistry and high, modular electrical conductivity.1 – 5 These remarkable properties enable application of the compounds as thermoelectrics,6 chemical sensors,7 electrocatalysts,8 field-effect transistors,3 and energy storage materials.5,9,10 Nevertheless, despite this tremendous importance and versatility, the synthesis of crystalline frameworks with amidic linkers represents a daunting synthetic challenge. Specifically, amino protons are much less acidic than hydroxo protons,11 and consequently, upon deprotonation, their conjugate bases tend to form strong and relatively covalent metal-amide bonds. Since reversible coordination is critical to the formation of crystalline framework materials, very little progress has been made toward synthesizing compounds with amidic linkers. Indeed, among the small number of amidic frameworks reported to date, including a recent report of microcrystals of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}$ (hexaiminotriphenylene)2,12 none of them have been characterized by single-crystal X-ray diffraction.1-5
As a strategy to surmount this synthetic challenge, one can envision employing post-synthetic linker exchange, which has emerged as a powerful tool to synthesize frameworks that are otherwise inaccessible through direct synthesis. 13 – 19 Historically, through linker exchange, functionalities that are not tolerant to solvothermal conditions have been successfully incorporated onto framework materials.20–23 For example, a myriad of catalytic units embedded on organic linkers have been introduced post-synthetically to imbue a range of catalytic function.24–27 In addition, linker exchange has recently been utilized to construct heterostructures with multiple domains distributed macroscopically within each crystal.28–31 Despite its extraordinary ability to generate new materials and superstructures, linker exchange has thus far been almost exclusively limited to installing linkers with the same donors as the original linker, and has only been observed for carboxylateor $N_{\sun}$ -heterocycle-based linkers.13–27 Indeed, exchange reactions involving linkers with different coordinating atoms or across several $\mathrm{{\ttp}}K\mathrm{{a}}$ units remain unreported.
Inspired by the above examples, we sought to employ post-synthetic linker exchange as a strategy to install amidic linkers into frameworks. To illustrate this approach, we chose parent materials based on $^{2,5}$ -dihydroxo-1,4-benzoquinone linkers, which can be readily produced as large single crystals.32–46 Upon standing in hot DMF over the course of days, these compounds partially dissolve. In principle, this slow dissolution process should lend itself to substitution by an exogenous linker that forms stronger metal-ligand bonds. Specifically, such a process should provide a route to introduce amidic benzoquinoid linkers into the solid-state structure.
Herein, we report just such a series of linker exchange reactions. The parent framework compounds $\left(\mathrm{Me_{2}N H_{2}}\right)_{2}\left[\mathrm{M_{2}L_{3}}\right]$ $\mathbf{\tilde{M}}=\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}_{\mathbf{\cdot}}$ , Mn; H2L $^{\circ}=2,5$ -dichloro- $^{3,6}$ -dihydroxo-1,4-benzoquinone) undergo linker exchange upon exposure to a solution of deprotonated 2,5-diamino-3,6- dibromo-1,4-benzoquinone $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{{Br}}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}\right)$ or 2,5-diamino-3,6-dichloro-1,4- benzoquinone $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{Cl}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}\right)$ . These processes occur via single-crystal-tosingle-crystal reactions to afford four structurally-characterized metaldiamidobenzoquinone framework compounds. The identities of the linkers in these frameworks were confirmed by NMR, Raman, and energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) spectroscopies. To quantify the linker exchange in the $Z\mathrm{n}$ frameworks, we developed a facile quantitative $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectroscopic method using standard pulse sequence and external calibration curves. This work provides a method to exchange organic linkers with different donor atoms and acidities differing by up to
$11\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ in MOFs, which may lead to new materials with exotic metallinker coordination bonds and unusual physical properties.36,40,44,46–51
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
The compounds $(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{3}]$ (1) and $(\mathbf{Me}_{2}\mathbf{NH}_{2})_{2}[\mathbf{Mn}_{2}\mathbf{L}_{3}]$ (4) were selected as parent materials from which to carry out post-synthetic linker exchange. Compound 1 was prepared according to a previously reported procedure,36 whereas 4 was synthesized as brown, hexagonal crystals through the reaction of $\displaystyle{\bf M n}\big({\bf N O}_{3}\big)_{2}$ with $_\mathrm{H_{2}L}$ in DMF at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $16\mathrm{h}$ . Single-crystal X-ray diffraction analysis indicated that 4 is isostructural to 1 (see Figure 1a), featuring eclipsed, stacked layers of $\scriptstyle{\big[}\mathbf{Mn}_{2}\mathbf{L}_{3}{\big]}^{2-}$ honeycomb networks that are charge balanced with interstitial $\mathrm{(Me_{2}N H_{2})^{+}}$ counterions located in the hexagonal channels (see Figure S1).
In an initial attempt to carry out linker exchange, crystals of 1 or 4 were soaked in a DMF solution of $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{\tiny~Br}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}$ or $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{Cl}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}$ . However, after prolonged reaction time at room temperature or elevated temperature, no diaminobenzoquinone linkers were incorporated. Instead, significant dissolution of the crystals was observed. Since values of $\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{al}}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L},\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{\tiny~Br}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{~Cl}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}$ were determined to be 4.7, 16.1 and 12.3, respectively, in DMF (see discussion of exchange mechanism below), we surmise that dimethylamine, generated by DMF hydrolysis, is not sufficiently basic to deprotonate the diaminobenzoquinone ligands and that ligand exchange through protonolysis is similarly unfavorable. We therefore prepared DMF solutions of monodeprotonated $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{\tiny~Br}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}$ or $\mathrm{{H}}_{2}^{\mathrm{{.cl}}}\mathrm{{L}}^{\prime}$ by addition of one equivalent of $\left(\mathrm{Et4N}\right)\mathrm{OH}$ to give mono-deprotonated linkers $(\mathrm{H^{Br}L^{\prime}})^{-}$ and $(\mathrm{H^{Cl}L^{\prime}})^{-}$ . Soaking MOF crystals in these solutions at $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for three days afforded hexagonal crystals of $\mathrm{\big(Et_{4}N\big)_{2}[Z n_{2}L_{1.3}^{\mathrm{{\tiny~Br}}}L_{1.7}^{\prime}].0.57D M F.0.14E t_{2}O}$ (2), $\mathrm{\small{\left(Et_{4}N\right)_{2}}[Z n_{2}L_{1.1}{}^{C l}L_{1.9}^{\prime}]}.0.64\mathrm{CH_{2}C l_{2}}$ (3, see Figure 1c), $\mathrm{(EtN)_{2}[M n_{2}L_{1.8}^{\mathrm{{Br}}}L_{1.2}^{\prime}]}.0.65\mathrm{DMF}\ (\pmb{5}$ ), or $\mathrm{\big(Et{4N}\big)}_{2}\big[\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{1.5}{}^{\mathrm{Cl}}\mathrm{L}_{1.5}^{\prime}\big]$ (6), which were all suitable for single-crystal X-ray diffraction analysis. Notably, these linker exchanges proceed via single-crystal-to-single-crystal (SC-SC) reactions. In contrast, addition of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ or $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}$ to the equimolar solution of $(\mathrm{Et4N})\mathrm{OH}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{\tiny{Br}}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}$ (or $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{~Cl}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}$ ) at room temperature invariably resulted in the immediate precipitation of amorphous solids.
Attempts to directly synthesize the amidic MOFs through solvothermal methods without the addition of $\left(\mathrm{Et4N}\right)\mathrm{OH}$ produced no solid products. In addition, solvothermal synthesis with $\mathbf{M}^{2+}$ $\mathrm{\bf~\langleM=Zn,}$ Mn) and $\mathrm{H_{2}^{\mathrm{~X}}L^{\prime}(X=C l,B r)}$ with the addition of $_\mathrm{H_{2}L}$ solely produced 1 and 4 without incorporation of $\mathrm{\cdotH_{2}^{X}L^{\prime}}$ , as determined by Raman spectroscopy and 13CNMR spectroscopy on acid-digested samples (see relevant discussions below). These observations eliminate the possibility of a dissolution-recrystallization mechanism, and they underscore the necessity of post-synthetic linker exchange to obtain these kinetic products.
The addition of one equivalent of $\left(\mathrm{Et4N}\right)\mathrm{OH}$ per molecule of linker in these exchange reactions corresponds to an optimized stoichiometry based on both pH (see below) and solubility considerations. The solubility of both $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{~X~}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}\left(\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{Br},\mathrm{Cl}\right)$ and $(\mathrm{Et}_{4}\mathrm{N})_{2}^{\mathrm{X}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}$ is low in DMF, whereas the solubility of mono-deprotonated $\mathrm{(Et_{4}N)H^{X}L^{\prime}}$ can reach $0.15\mathrm{mM}$ in DMF. Considering the relative $\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ values of $\mathrm{(Me_{2}N H_{2})^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{X}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime},$ , some protonation of $(\mathrm{H^{x}L^{\prime}})^{-}$ is expected over the course of the ligand exchange, with concomitant counterion exchange of $\mathrm{(Me_{2}N H_{2})^{+}}$ for $\mathrm{(Et_{4}N)^{+}}$ . As such, a high initial concentration of $\mathrm{(Et_{4}N)H^{X}L^{\prime}}$ is desired. Lowering the stoichiometry of $\left(\mathrm{Et4N}\right)\mathrm{OH}$ resulted in the incomplete dissolution of $\mathrm{\cdotH_{2}^{\mathrm{\tiny~X}}L^{\prime}}$ and a lower concentration of $\mathrm{(Et_{4}N)H^{X}L^{\prime}}$ , thereby slowing the linker exchange. Addition of additional $\mathrm{Et_{4}N O H}$ (between one and two equivalents) resulted in the precipitation of the linker as insoluble $(\mathrm{Et}_{4}\mathrm{N})_{2}^{\mathrm{X}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}.$ Finally, slow addition of excess $\left(\mathrm{Et4N}\right)\mathrm{OH}$ (more than two equivalents) over the course of the exchange reaction resulted in framework dissolution and formation of an amorphous precipitate.
The single-crystal X-ray structures of 2, 3, 5, and 6 reveal that the compounds belong to the hexagonal space group $P\overline{{31}}m$ . Similar to 1 and 4, these structures feature eclipsed, stacked anionic layers with a honeycomb topology (see Figure 1a). Both the exchanged $(^{\mathrm{X}}\mathrm{L^{\prime}})^{2-}$ and the parent linkers $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ occupy the same lattice sites with partial occupancies. During the linker exchange reactions, the $\mathrm{(Me_{2}N H_{2})^{+}}$ counterions in 1 and 4 are replaced by $\mathrm{(Et_{4}N)^{+}}$ . As discussed above, this counterion exchange is likely driven by deprotonation of the weakly acidic $\mathrm{(Me_{2}N H_{2})^{+}}$ by the basic $\left(\mathrm{Et4N}\right)\mathrm{H^{X}L^{\prime}}$ to form $\bf{M e}_{2}\bf{N H}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{X}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}$ . Following linker exchange, the $\mathrm{(Et.N)^{+}}$ ion resides equidistant between the two nearest metal ions from two adjacent layers (see Figures S2– S5). These linker exchange reactions cause an elongation of both $a$ and c unit cell axes (see Table 1), with more significant changes in 2 and 3 compared to 5 and 6 (see Table 1).
The lengthening of unit cell parameter a conveniently allows the linker exchange to be followed by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD). As shown in Figure 2 (right, expanded views), linker-exchanged 2, 3, 5, and 6 feature a pronounced shift in $2\theta$ toward lower angles relative to parent 1 and 4, consistent with the elongation of a. For instance, the ${<}100>$ peak moved from $7.445^{\circ}$ to $7.295^{\circ}$ or $7.265^{\circ}$ from 1 to 2 or 3, respectively, corresponding to elongation of a from $13.70\mathring\mathrm{A}$ to $13.98\mathring\mathrm{A}$ or $14.04\mathring{\mathrm{A}}.$ Similarly, from 4 to 5 or 6, the $<100>$ peak moved from $7.280^{\circ}$ to $7.145^{\circ}$ or $7.115^{\circ}$ , respectively, corresponding an elongation of a from 14.01 Å to $14.27\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ or $14.33\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ . These unit cell elongations are consistent between single crystal and powder X-ray diffraction data.
The change of the interlayer distance as a consequence of counterion exchange is also reflected by the shifting of the $<001>$ peak toward lower angles. From 1 to 2 or 3, the $<001>$ peak moved from $9.425^{\circ}$ to $8.405^{\circ}$ or $8.390^{\circ}.$ , respectively, corresponding to the change in $c$ from $9.38\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ to $10.51\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ or $10.53\mathring\mathrm{A}.$ . A similar trend was observed from 4 to 5 or 6. Note that the $c$ parameter obtained from the $<001>$ peaks of PXRD analysis are slightly larger than those obtained from single-crystal diffraction analysis due to differences in temperature between the two techniques. Nevertheless, the same trends are observed in both analyses. Importantly, these bulk measurements suggest that the exchanged frameworks are all pure, single-phase materials (see Figures S6–S9), rather than macroscopically-separated crystalline domains with distinct chemical compositions as have been observed in manganese-benzoquinoid framework crystals with partial metal exchange.40
Amine-substituted benzoquinoid molecules are prone to hydrolysis under hydrothermal conditions.52,53 This tendency has been exploited to deliberately synthesize the 3D framework compounds $\left(\mathrm{Bu}_{4}\mathrm{N}\right)_{2}\left[\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{dh}\mathrm{bq})_{3}\right]$ $\mathrm{\dot{M}=M n,}$ , Fe, Co, Ni, Zn, Cd; $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{dhbq}=2,5$ -dihydroxo-1,4-benzoquinone) via in situ hydrolysis.50,51 Nevertheless, under the linker exchange conditions reported here, hydrolysis was definitively not observed for $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{\tiny{Br}}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}$ or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{^{Cl}L^{\prime}}$ , as demonstrated by Raman spectroscopy, $^{13}{\mathrm{CNMR}}$ spectroscopy, and combustion elemental analysis.
First, Raman spectroscopy indicates that vibrations associated with both $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ and $(^{\mathrm{X}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime})^{2-}\left(\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{Cl},\mathrm{Br}\right)$ are present in 2, 3, 5, and 6. As shown in Figure 3, the bands at $1346{-}1364\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (orange) are assigned to an averaged C–C ring stretch of L2−.36 The corresponding C–C ring vibrations for $(^{\mathrm{{Br}}}\mathrm{{L^{\prime}}})^{2-}$ and $(^{\mathrm{Cl}}\mathrm{L^{\prime}})^{2-}$ appear at $1369{-}1373~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (blue) and $1389~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ (green), respectively. Additionally, the Raman bands at $1602{-}1612\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (purple) are assigned to the C–O stretching vibration of L2−,36 whereas the features at $1532{-}1544\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (pink) are assigned to the collective $\mathrm{C-N/C-O}$ stretching vibration for $(^{\mathrm{Br}}\mathrm{L^{\prime}})^{2-}$ and $(^{\mathrm{Cl}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime})^{2-}$ . Upon linker exchange, a new band appears at $561{-}573~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (cyan), which we assign to the O–M–N $\left(\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Zn},\mathbf{Mn}\right)$ ) vibrations for $(^{\mathrm{{Br}}}\mathrm{{L^{\prime}}})^{2-}$ and $(^{\mathrm{Cl}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime})^{2-}$ . Finally, the O–M–O vibrations for $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ at $586{-}595~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ (gray) are preserved upon linker exchange.36
The linker identities were further corroborated by $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectroscopy of acid-digested samples of 1, 2, and 3. As shown in Figure 4, both $_\mathrm{H_{2}L}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{X}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}$ are present in solutions of acid-digested 2 and 3. In contrast, in the case that $\binom{\mathrm{Br}}{\mathrm{A}}^{2-}$ were hydrolyzed during MOF synthesis, one would expect the hydrolyzed linker 2,5-dibromo-3,6-dihydroxo-1,4-benzoquinone instead of $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{2}^{B r}L^{\prime}}$ to be present in the solution of 2, and similarly, only H2L peaks would be present in 3.
To quantify the extent of linker exchange in the reactions involving Zn frameworks, we developed a facile quantitative $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR method using a standard pulse sequence.54 Since the transverse relaxation time constant $\left(T_{2}\right)$ for the $^{13}{\mathrm{C}}$ nucleus is highly dependent on chemical environment and is longer than the $0.4\:s$ relaxation delay defined by the standard pulse sequence, quantitation of $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ atoms with different chemical environments is precluded. Although it is possible to extend the relaxation delay at a cost of significantly prolonged acquisition time, we decided to instead establish external calibration curves by acquiring $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectra for a series of solution mixtures of $_\mathrm{H_{2}L}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{\tiny~Br}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}$ or $_\mathrm{H_{2}L}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{~Cl}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}$ . Peaks corresponding to each ligand were integrated, and the fractional peak area of $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}^{\mathrm{Br}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}$ or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{^{Cl}L}$ with respect to total peak area of both ligands (i.e. the $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR index) was plotted against their mole fractions. As shown in Figures 4b and 4c, excellent linear relationships were obtained for both of these linker mixtures. Finally, these external calibration curves enable us to quantify linker exchange by calculating the $^{13}{\mathrm{CNMR}}$ index from a standard, qualitative $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectrum of an acid-digested sample of the corresponding compound (Figures S10-11). As a result of this analysis, the mole fractions of the exchanged linkers $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{Br}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{~Cl}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}$ were determined to be $58(1)\%$ and $64.8\AA^{3}\%$ in 2 and 3, respectively, establishing the composition of the anionic layers as $[\mathrm{Zn_{2}L_{1.3}}^{\mathrm{Br}}\mathrm{L^{\prime}_{1.7}}]^{2-}$ and $[\mathrm{Zn_{2}L_{1.1}}^{\mathrm{Cl}}\mathrm{L^{\prime}}_{1.9}]^{2-}$ . Additionally, quantitation of the occluded solvent molecules by $\mathrm{^{1}H N M R}$ provides overall chemical formulas of $\mathrm{\big(Et_{4}N\big)_{2}[Z n_{2}L_{1.3}^{\mathrm{\tiny~Br}}L_{1.7}^{\prime}]}\cdot0.57\mathrm{DMF}\cdot0.14\mathrm{Et_{2}O}$ and $\mathrm{\small{\left(Et_{4}N\right)_{2}}[Z n_{2}L_{1.1}{}^{C l}L_{1.9}^{\prime}]}{\cdot0.64C H_{2}C l_{2}}$ for 2 and 3, respectively (see Figures S12 and S13). These chemical formulas are further corroborated by combustion elemental analysis (see Table 1 and Experimental Section).
To probe the spatial distribution of linkers in the parent and exchanged frameworks, we performed mapping and line scan experiments using EDX and Raman spectroscopies for 5 and 3, respectively. First, EDX mapping (see Figures S16–20) and line scans (see Figure 5c, e, f) for 5 indicate a homogenous distribution of Br and Cl atoms along both the ab plane and the c axis. Similarly, Raman mapping and line scans for 3 reveal a constant intensity ratio between $1550\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (C–N for $\mathrm{{^{Cl}L^{2-}}}$ ) and $1612\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (C–O for $\operatorname{L}^{2-}$ ) across the entire crystal (see Figure 6).31 These spatially resolved EDX and Raman spectroscopy studies indicate that both the parent $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ and diamido $(^{\mathrm{{Br}}}\mathrm{{L^{\prime}}})^{2-}$ or $(^{\mathrm{Cl}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime})^{2-}$ linkers are homogenously distributed within linker-exchanged MOF crystals on the mesoscopic to macroscopic length scale, further precluding sample heterogeneity caused by the diffusion barriers. Since linker exchange does not proceed further by subjecting linker-exchanged crystals to additional linker exchange conditions, these mixedlinker frameworks likely represent thermodynamically metastable products.
To gain some insight into the mechanism of linker exchange, we first carried out acid-base titrations of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L},\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{\tiny~Br}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime},$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{~Cl}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}$ in DMF. As shown in Figure S21, all three linkers exhibit two distinct deprotonation processes at well-isolated $\mathrm{\pH}$ regions. The corresponding first deprotonation process can be expressed as follows:
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{[\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{2}}\mathrm{Ligand}]\rightleftharpoons[\mathrm{H}^{+}]+[\mathrm{HLigand}^{-}]}\\ &{K_{\mathrm{a1}}=[\mathrm{H}^{+}][\mathrm{HLigand}^{-}]/[\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{2}}\mathrm{Ligand}]}\end{array}
$$
For weak acids in DMF, the assumption that $\left[\mathrm{H}^{+}\right]=\left[\mathrm{HLigand}^{-}\right]<<$ [H2Ligand] can be made, such that $\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{al}}$ values can be calculated according to the following expression:
$$
\mathrm{\p}K_{\mathrm{al}}=-\mathrm{log}\big(\big[\mathrm{H}^{+}\big]^{2}/\big[\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Ligand}\big]\big)
$$
This analysis gives $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{al}}$ values of 4.7, 16.1 and 12.3 for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L},\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{Br}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime}$ , and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{^{cl}L^{\prime}}$ , respectively, in DMF.
Based on these values of $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{al}}$ , upon addition of one equivalent $\left(\mathrm{Et_{4}N}\right)\mathrm{OH}$ to a suspension of $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}\mathrm{\cdotL}^{\prime}$ $\mathrm{\DeltaX=Br}$ , Cl) in DMF, the ligand becomes deprotonated and consequently solubilized to give $\mathrm{(Et_{4}N)^{+}(H^{\mathrm{X}}L^{\prime})^{-}}$ at a concentration of $0.15\ \mathrm{mM}.$ . Counterion exchange is then driven by the deprotonation of $\begin{array}{r l}{\lefteqn{\big(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)^{+}}}\end{array}$ :
$$
\mathrm{\Gamma(Me_{2}N H_{2})^{+}+E t_{4}N(H^{X}L^{\prime})\rightarrow(E t_{4}N)^{+}+H_{2}^{X}L^{\prime}+M e_{2}N H}
$$
While this acid-base reaction will consume a stoichiometric amount of solubilized $\mathrm{(H^{X}L^{\prime})^{-}}$ , the large excess of linker relative to pre-exchanged framework ensures that a high concentration of $(\mathrm{H^{x}L^{\prime}})^{-}$ is present in solution and can thus participate in linker exchange.
To obtain a snapshot of counterion and linker exchange processes, we halted the reaction from 1 to 3 after 15 hours and collected a PXRD pattern of the product. As shown in Figure S22, the diffractogram displays two ${<}001>$ peaks at $8.375^{\circ}$ and $8.465^{\circ}.$ , corresponding to two distinct $\boldsymbol{c}$ lattice parameters and thus interlayer distances of $\mathrm{i}0.55\mathrm{\AA}$ and $10.44\mathring{\mathrm{A}}.$ . These distances correspond to the full and partially counterion-exchanged phases, respectively, and they match much more closely with the $\mathrm{(Et.N)^{+}}$ phases than the $\mathrm{(Me_{2}N H_{2})^{+}}$ phases. This suggests that counterion exchange is nearly complete after $15\mathrm{~h~}$ . Conversely, two ${<}100>$ peaks at $7.250^{\circ}$ and $7.415^{\circ}$ reveal a different scenario. The ${<}100{>}$ peak at $7.250^{\circ}$ corresponding to a lattice parameter a of $14.07\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ indicates the presence of a mixed-linker framework phase, whereas the other ${<}100{>}$ peak at $7.415^{\circ}$ is consistent with a lattice a parameter of $13.76\mathrm{~\AA~}$ that corresponds to the parent $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{3}]^{2-}$ framework. From these data, we deduce that counterion exchange proceeds faster than does linker exchange, as would be expected for a simple acid-base reaction.
The observation of two distinct $a$ lattice parameters, seemingly corresponding to the initial framework and the exchange-saturated framework, suggests the exchange process may be kinetically limited by linker diffusion through the pores of the framework. We envision that linker exchange initiates at the exterior of a crystal and gradually propagates toward the center. This mechanism implies that local linker exchange rate is much faster than linker diffusion such that phase segregation within a single crystal is anticipated at intermediate levels of exchange. Similar diffusion-limited phase segregation has been observed during the metal exchange process of $\left(\mathrm{Et}_{4}\mathrm{N}\right)_{2}\left[\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{3}\right]$ with $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ or $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ .40 The appearance of two ${<}100{>}$ reflections could also be suggestive of a cooperative exchange phenomena in which a small amount of ligand exchange promotes rapid exchange within a single two-dimensional layer. While diffusion effects are more likely to explain the observed data, both effects could be operative in the materials described here. Further studies of crystallites at intermediate exchange are necessary to better understand the macroscopic exchange mechanism in these framework materials.
Regarding the local linker exchange mechanism, we propose a dissociative mechanism (see Figure S23).55 The observation of framework dissolution upon extended soaking in hot DMF indicates relatively dynamic metal–linker coordination at these conditions. Upon dissociation of $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ at one metal site, the deprotonated N atom of $(\mathrm{H^{x}L^{\prime}})^{-}$ can bind irreversibly. Subsequent dissociation of $\cdot^{\cdot_{\mathrm{~L~}^{2-}}}$ from the second metal site should lead to preferential formation of a bridging $(^{\mathrm{X}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime})^{2-}$ linker, in which deprotonation of $(\mathrm{H^{\mathrm{{x}}}L^{\prime}})^{-}$ by $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ is enabled by the reduced $\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ of $(\mathrm{H^{x}L^{\prime}})^{-}$ upon coordination to a metal ion.
An explanation for the values of exchange saturation is more challenging to propose. The maximum amount of $(^{\mathrm{X}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime})^{2-}$ being incorporated varies between framework metal and linker identities. The dissociative mechanism proposed above involves dynamic binding of $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ . Dissociation of the ligand from one metal ion requires some structural flexibility of the crystal lattice, which may involve the simultaneous dissociation of multiple ligands across the 2D layer. In contrast, the more covalent M– $(\mathrm{^XL^{\prime}})^{2-}$ linkage is expected to provide static, irreversible binding. As the linker exchange proceeds towards saturation, local strain from linker disorder and the increasing lattice parameters, paired with the reduced concentration of dynamic $\mathrm{L}^{2-}$ linkers, may inhibit the lattice dynamics that are required to observe complete linker exchange. In support of this hypothesis, the observation of high-angle peak broadening in the PXRD data of the linker-exchanged materials (see Figure 2) suggests the presence of increased strain of the framework lattice.44 We note that other effects, such as hydrogen bonding interactions involving the protons of $(^{\mathrm{X}}\mathrm{L}^{\prime})^{2-}$ , may also play a role in reducing lattice dynamics and arresting linker exchange in these materials.
Increased lattice strain is likely to destabilize framework materials upon linker exchange, and this proposal is supported by the findings from gas adsorption experiments. According to $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption and desorption isotherms collected at $77\mathrm{~K~}$ (see Figure S23), 1 exhibits a Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of $610~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (see Figure S24). Conversely, compounds 3-6 do not exhibit permanent porosity upon solvent removal (see SI for activation procedures). In the case of Zn, this loss of permanent porosity may result from lattice strain associated with linker exchange, particularly considering the tendency of this family of frameworks to undergo structural changes upon desolvation.36 Specifically, lattice strain induced by linker disorder may lead to more substantial changes in the local coordination environment during activation. The lack of permanent porosity for all Mn-containing compounds is likely due to the relative flexibility of the Mn coordination sphere. Note that the related compound $\big(\mathrm{Et}_{4}\mathrm{N}\big)_{2}\big[\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{3}\big].$ , was also shown to exhibit no permanent porosity when activated using a similar procedure.42
These preliminary mechanistic studies illustrate the potential for exchanging linkers with different donor atoms and with very different pKa values. Further studies of frameworks at different levels of linker exchange and of the local coordination of the linker-exchanged frameworks will provide additional insights into the mechanism of linker exchange and the origin of incomplete exchange in these frameworks. We envision this base-assisted linker exchange procedure could be generalized to a broad scope of MOFs and linkers if the following criteria are met: 1) the new linker should increase the metal–ligand bond strength to provide an energetic driving force for the reaction; 2) the new linker should be less acidic than the starting linker to favor irreversible exchange under basic conditions; and 3) the framework should possess dynamic and flexible metal–ligand coordination to enable associative or dissociative exchange processes.
## SUMMARY AND OUTLOOK
The foregoing results report a synthetic method that provides members of an exceedingly rare class of amidic MOFs. Specifically, this method expands the scope of post-synthetic linker exchange to introduce linkers with coordinating atoms of dramatically different basicity. This synthetic route harnesses the relatively irreversible coordination bonds, which typically lead to amorphous solids, to synthesize crystals that are not accessible through direct synthesis. This work may exemplify a general synthetic route toward frameworks without reversible coordination bonds and those with exotic metal-linker coordination modes. Further, this strategy could provide a new synthetic route to frameworks with increased metal–ligand covalency for enhanced electronic and magnetic properties. We envision that this strategy should be generally applicable to the synthesis of new frameworks with enhanced metal-ligand covalency, potentially including the exchange of imine or thiolene for oxolene linkers and amidate or thiocarboxylate for carboxylate linkers. The increased metal–ligand covalency afforded by this approach, paired with the single-crystal-to-single-crystal transformations, may facilitate the growth of large single crystals of highly covalent frameworks for electronic transport and magnetic measurements. Future work will aim to explore the physical properties of these linker-exchanged frameworks and to expand the scope of this strategy to other families of frameworks, to materials with metal-phosphine and metal-carbon linkages, and to frameworks which undergo quantitative linker exchange.
